  "query_preview": "..\n\nSaturday through Friday...\n\nDry cold front will move through the forecast through the early\nmonring hours, bringing a period of gusty (20-40mph, locally \nstronger Musselshell to Fallon county) northwest winds to the \nregion, and cooler more seasonal temperatures for the weekend. At \nthis point not seeing any measurable precipitation with this \nsystem as the main energy and push of moisture tracks northeast \naway from our area. Highs on Saturday look very pleasant around 70\ndegrees with sunny skies and diminishing wind in the afternoon. \n\nNext system set to impact the region arrives late Sunday. Ahead of\nthis system expect quiet weather across the region with continued\nseasonal temperatures, but an increase in cloud cover Sunday. That\nsaid, during the day atmospheric moisture levels will climb as low\nlevel southeast winds advect plains moisture into the area,\nboosting PWATs above an inch east of Billings Sunday night through\nMonday night. So, there is significant moisture available for a\nstorm system to utilize late this weekend into early next week.\nThe problem is that the west coast system that is forecast to move\ninto the region is splitting its energy, sending the vast majority\nof the forcing around us. As a result, precipitation probabilities\nand amounts are trending downward run to run in the models. The\nlatest NBM (which plays catchup to the model runs, so is a bit\nbehind in lowering precipitation chances) shows the potential for\na quarter of an inch of precipitation Sunday night into Tuesday\nmorning confined to areas near the eastern state line at around a\n50% probability. For most of the forecast area the probability \nfor a tenth of an inch is sitting under 20%. Probabilities for\nsnow have plummeted in the western mountains as well, with chances\nfor an inch or more during the event sitting at 30% and that is\nabove 10.5kft. If that holds, impacts to travel or activities in\nthe mountains will be minor. \n\nDry period builds in with zonal flow aloft keeping sunny skies and\nbreezy afternoons over the region Tuesday through Thursday. Next\npotential storm system is being advertised for next weekend. The\nrub is that only about 40% of ensemble members are advertising a\nstorm system of any strength during this period. 20% are sliding\nan Alberta Clipper type trof into the Northern Plains/Great Lakes\nbypassing our area to the north/east, and the remaining 40% of\nensembles including a whopping 76% of ECMWF members keep a\nwarm/dry zonal flow pattern transitioning to a ridge over the area\nFriday-Sunday. So, not a lot of confidence in the far extended\nforecasts. That makes sense given the chaotic nature of numerous\ntropical systems roaming the Pacific, impacting the upper pattern\n(tele-connections). For now advertising temperatures in the 60s\nwith a chance of lower elevation precipitation, and a chance for\nsnow in the mountains Friday into the weekend, but expect\npotentially significant changes as the NBM picks up on the\nuncertainty within a majority of todays ensemble model runs.\nChambers",
